|Tile that can be destroyed when bumped from beneath and Mario is not in small form||\r\n4|Question Mark Block|Tile that contains a special surprise (power-up) when bumped from beneath||\r\n5|Tube|Used as platforms or \u0022tunnels\u0022 to other sub-levels||\r\n6|Coin|Collectible coin|0|5\r\n7|Gomba|Enemy which dies from jumping on top of them||\r\n8|Koopa|Enemy which when it dies from jumping on top of them, they will proceed to leave a shell which will move horizontally and bounce back and forth on wall||\r\n9|Spiny|Enemy which cannot be killed from jumping on top of them||\r\n","Width":"50","Height":"16","GameType":"Side Scroller","GameGenre":"Platform","DifficultyLevel":"Medium","HazardLevel":"Easy","CustomConstraints":"The level **must** have unbroken pipes (pipes are in groups of 2 tiles side by side)\n\nThe level **must** have 50% or more of just flat platform to comply with mario distribution of tiles\n\nThere **must** not be \u0022floating\u0022 enemies\n\nThe level **must** be solvable by an A* agent\n\nThe level **must** have between 0 and 10 jumps \n\nThe total number of enemies **must always** be between 0 and 7","TemplateFilePath":"PromptV1.hbs"},"mario-small-v0_7":{"GameName":"Super Mario Bros","GameDescription":"Super Mario Bros. is a platform game in which the player controls the titular protagonist Mario, who is tasked with exploring the Mushroom Kingdom to defeat Bowser and rescue Princess Toadstool. His brother, Luigi, is controlled by the second player in multiplayer mode and assumes the same plot role and functionality as Mario.\u200A The game takes place through a side-scrolling perspective where the player moves to the right to reach the flagpole at the end of each level.","LevelName":"smbtile-v0","LevelDescription":"","Tiles":"\r\nTile Character|Tile Name|Tile Description|Minimum Number Of Tile|Maximum Number Of Tiles\r\n---|---|---|---|---\r\n0|Empty|Background tile||\r\n1|Floor|Walkable floor||\r\n2|Ladder|Solid ladder tile||\r\n3|Breakable Brick|Tile that can be destroyed when bumped from beneath and Mario is not in small form||\r\n4|Question Mark Block|Tile that contains a special surprise (power-up) when bumped from beneath||\r\n5|Tube|Used as platforms or \u0022tunnels\u0022 to other sub-levels||\r\n6|Coin|Collectible coin|0|5\r\n7|Gomba|Enemy which dies from jumping on top of them||\r\n8|Koopa|Enemy which when it dies from jumping on top of them, they will proceed to leave a shell which will move horizontally and bounce back and forth on wall||\r\n9|Spiny|Enemy which cannot be killed from jumping on top of them||\r\n","Width":"50","Height":"16","GameType":"Side Scroller","GameGenre":"Platform","DifficultyLevel":"Medium","HazardLevel":"Easy","CustomConstraints":"The level **must** have unbroken pipes (pipes are in groups of 2 tiles side by side)\n\nThe level **must** have 50% or more of just flat platform to comply with mario distribution of tiles\n\nThere **must** not be \u0022floating\u0022 enemies\n\nThe level **must** be solvable by an A* agent\n\nThe level **must** have between 0 and 10 jumps \n\nThe total number of enemies **must always** be between 0 and 7","TemplateFilePath":"PromptV1.hbs"},"zelda-v0_0":{"GameName":"Zelda","GameDescription":"SuperThe zelda problem was introduced originally throught the GVGAI framework. The problem is a bit more complicated than generating a maze as there has to be connectivity and specific number of items on the map which made it get a lot of research attraction and used in many papers (\u0022Path of Destruction\u0022, \u0022PCGRL: Procedural Content Generation via Reinforcement Learning\u0022, \u0022Bootstrapping conditional gans for video game level generation\u0022). The problem is just a simple dungeon crawler where the player need to get a key and go to the door without dying from the enemies. The goal of the problem is to generate a fully connected playable level with enemies.","LevelName":"zelda-v0","LevelDescription":"","Tiles":"\r\nTile Character|Tile Name|Tile Description|Minimum Number Of Tile|Maximum Number Of Tiles\r\n---|---|---|---|---\r\n0|Wall|Solid impassable wall||\r\n1|Floor|Walkable floor||\r\n2|Player|The player\u0027s starting position|1|1\r\n3|Key|Key needed to unlock the exit door|1|1\r\n4|Exit Door|The end of the level|1|1\r\n5|Enemy|The end of the level|2|4\r\n","Width":"11","Height":"7","GameType":"Top Down","GameGenre":"Dungeon Crawler","DifficultyLevel":"Medium","HazardLevel":"Easy","CustomConstraints":"The solution length **must** take at least 18 steps\n\nThe amount of steps from the starting position to the key **must** be between 9 and 19\n\nThe amount of steps from the key to the door **must** be between 9 and 19","TemplateFilePath":"PromptV1.hbs"},"zelda-v0_1":{"GameName":"Zelda","GameDescription":"SuperThe zelda problem was introduced originally throught the GVGAI framework. The problem is a bit more complicated than generating a maze as there has to be connectivity and specific number of items on the map which made it get a lot of research attraction and used in many papers (\u0022Path of Destruction\u0022, \u0022PCGRL: Procedural Content Generation via Reinforcement Learning\u0022, \u0022Bootstrapping conditional gans for video game level generation\u0022). The problem is just a simple dungeon crawler where the player need to get a key and go to the door without dying from the enemies. The goal of the problem is to generate a fully connected playable level with enemies.","LevelName":"zelda-v0","LevelDescription":"","Tiles":"\r\nTile Character|Tile Name|Tile Description|Minimum Number Of Tile|Maximum Number Of Tiles\r\n---|---|---|---|---\r\n0|Wall|Solid impassable wall||\r\n1|Floor|Walkable floor||\r\n2|Player|The player\u0027s starting position|1|1\r\n3|Key|Key needed to unlock the exit door|1|1\r\n4|Exit Door|The end of the level|1|1\r\n5|Enemy|The end of the level|2|4\r\n","Width":"11","Height":"7","GameType":"Top Down","GameGenre":"Dungeon Crawler","DifficultyLevel":"Mediu